XGraph
======

XGraph is an XS-backed directed graph for XPerl.  It is intended for graph
topology and graph algorithms, not for drawing graphics or storing arbitrary
application objects.

XGraph is bundled in XPerl's ``dist/XGraph`` tree.  The separate development
repository is useful for building and testing the native representation.
XGraph is not currently a CPAN release.

XS is the bridge that lets a Perl module call C code.  XGraph uses C for the
topology and algorithms, then turns the results back into ordinary Perl
values.  This gives students two useful views of the same idea: the graph
algorithm in familiar Perl terms and the compact data structure underneath.

QUICK START
-----------

    use XGraph;

    my $g = XGraph->new(strict_labels => 1);
    $g->declare_label('depends_on');

    my $parse = $g->add_vertex('parse');
    my $emit  = $g->add_vertex('emit');
    my $tmp   = $g->add_vertex;              # anonymous vertex

    $g->add_edge_by_ids($parse, $emit, 'depends_on');

    print $g->has_path($parse, $emit) ? "reachable\n" : "not reachable\n";
    my $edges = $g->out_edges($parse);

The return value from ``add_vertex`` is the vertex's stable numeric ID.  Use
IDs for the native API, or use the name convenience methods when both
endpoints have names:

    $g->add_edge_by_names('parse', 'emit', 'depends_on');
    my $id = $g->vertex_id_by_name('parse');

GRAPH BASICS
------------

A graph is a collection of vertices and edges.  A vertex is a thing we want
to talk about.  An edge is a relationship between two vertices.  In XGraph,
edges are directed, which means that the arrow has a beginning and an end:

    parse  --depends_on-->  emit

The edge above says that 'parse' depends on 'emit'.  It does not
automatically say that 'emit' depends on 'parse'.  If both relationships
are needed, store both directed edges.

XGraph uses the word vertex where some books use node.  These words describe
the same idea.  A vertex ID is the machine-friendly identity.  A vertex name
is a human-friendly alias.  An unnamed vertex is still a perfectly useful
vertex because its ID is enough to identify it.

An edge has the value '(from ID, to ID, label)'.  The label describes the
kind of relationship.  It is not an arbitrary record attached to the edge.
For example, these are two different edges because their relationship kinds
are different:

    ($a, $b, 'depends_on')
    ($a, $b, 'generated_by')

The same three-part tuple cannot be inserted twice.  An omitted label means
the same thing as an 'undef' label.  The empty string is a different label.

Some useful graph vocabulary:

* The out-degree of a vertex is the number of edges leaving it.  The
  in-degree is the number entering it.
* A path is a sequence of vertices connected by edges.  A cycle is a path
  which returns to its starting vertex.
* A source has no incoming edges.  A sink has no outgoing edges.
* A directed acyclic graph, or DAG, has no directed cycle.  A topological
  ordering lists its vertices so every edge points forward in the list.
* A strongly connected component is a group in which every vertex can reach
  every other vertex.  A weakly connected component ignores arrow direction
  while deciding whether vertices belong to the same group.

The algorithm methods return IDs because IDs are unambiguous for both named
and anonymous vertices.  Use 'vertex($id)' or the name lookup methods when
you want a more descriptive Perl-facing view.

HOW THE IMPLEMENTATION REPRESENTS A GRAPH
------------------------------------------

The native graph keeps two sorted adjacency lists for each live vertex:

    vertex A, outgoing: (B, label)
    vertex B, incoming: (A, label)

Keeping both lists makes either direction quick to inspect.  It also means
that deleting an edge must remove two entries.  The entries contain only
integer IDs.  Names, labels, and vertex metadata stay in ordinary Perl AV/HV
structures at the boundary.  This separation keeps the frequently visited
topology compact and makes Perl reference ownership straightforward.

In Perl's C API, an SV is a scalar, an AV is an array, and an HV is a hash.
Those names appear in the XS and C sources because the graph uses Perl's
normal memory and reference-counting rules for this boundary data.

The compact build packs a neighboring vertex ID and a label ID into one
integer.  Because the lists are sorted by that packed value, binary search
can find an edge without a separate hash table.  The wide build uses the same
logical design with larger fields.  The packing is an implementation detail,
not a file format.

HOW TO READ THE ALGORITHMS
--------------------------

The path and reachability methods use breadth-first search.  Breadth-first
search visits all vertices one edge away, then all vertices two edges away,
and so on.  The first time it reaches a destination therefore gives a
shortest path measured in edges.  A queue holds the frontier, and a 'seen'
array prevents revisiting a vertex.

The depth-first methods use a stack.  They can report the order in which
vertices are first discovered (preorder), the order in which exploration
finishes (postorder), or the reverse of the finishing order.  The
implementation stores its own stack instead of using C recursion, so a deep
graph does not consume the C call stack one vertex at a time.

Topological sorting repeatedly chooses a vertex with no remaining incoming
edges.  Removing that vertex exposes new candidates.  If some vertices
remain when no candidate exists, those vertices are part of a cycle and no
topological order exists.

Strongly connected components use two depth-first passes.  The first records
finishing order.  The second walks the reversed edges in that order.  This is
Kosaraju's algorithm.  The condensation operation then replaces each
component with one vertex, producing a smaller acyclic graph.

DATA MODEL
----------

Every vertex has a graph-assigned numeric ID.  ID zero is invalid.  IDs start
at one, increase monotonically, and are not reused after a vertex is deleted.
This makes an ID safe to use as a key in an application-owned table.

Vertices may have unique string names.  An omitted name or ``undef`` creates
an anonymous vertex.  XGraph does not invent names for anonymous vertices.
The empty string is a valid name.  Names are stringified once and compared
using ordinary Perl hash-key semantics.  References are rejected.

An edge is the unique relationship tuple ``(from ID, to ID, label)``.  There
are no independent edge IDs.  Parallel edges are possible when their labels
differ.  An edge without a label and an edge labelled ``undef`` are the same
edge.  The empty string is a different label.

Labels are graph-local relationship names.  The graph interns them and keeps
the public label as a string.  The first two labels are reserved:

    label ID 1  undef
    label ID 2  ""

In loose mode, a new non-empty label is registered when an edge is added.  In
strict mode, non-empty labels must first be passed to ``declare_label`` or
``declare_labels``.  Label references are rejected.  A vertex may optionally
carry one arbitrary Perl metadata value; edges still carry only their
relationship labels, so richer edge records belong in a separate map keyed by
edge tuples.

BUILDING
--------

The normal developer build is:

    perl Makefile.PL
    make
    make test

The default native representation packs a 24-bit vertex ID and an 8-bit label
ID into each adjacency entry.  This permits vertex IDs from 1 through
``0xFFFFFF`` and up to 253 registered non-empty labels, in addition to the two
reserved labels.  The compact representation is an implementation detail,
not a serialization format.

The same tests can be run against the representation-equivalent wide build:

    make clean
    XGRAPH_WIDE=1 perl Makefile.PL
    make test

The wide build uses 32-bit vertex and label fields.  It is a compile-time
validation configuration, not a run-time option and not a promise that a
graph serialized by one build can be opened by the other.

API OVERVIEW
------------

Construction and lifecycle:

    XGraph->new(%options)
    XGraph->from_edges(%options)
    $g->freeze
    $g->is_frozen
    $g->clone
    $g->mutable_copy

The constructor accepts ``acyclic`` and ``strict_labels`` boolean options.
``freeze`` is irreversible and prevents changes to topology, names, labels,
and graph options.  ``clone`` makes an independent copy preserving frozen
state.  ``mutable_copy`` makes an independent mutable copy.

``from_edges`` is the bulk constructor.  Vertex references are names by
default; use ``vertex_mode => 'id'`` for numeric IDs.  It accepts flat
``edges`` pairs, label-grouped ``labeled_edges``, one or more ``paths``, and
label-grouped ``labeled_paths``.  Names are created on first use, and
duplicate edge tuples warn and are ignored.  Use ``vertices`` to declare
isolated vertices.

Vertices:

    add_vertex, has_vertex_id, vertex_id_by_name, vertex_name_by_id
    rename_vertex, delete_vertex_by_id, delete_vertex_by_name
    vertex_ids, vertices, vertex_count, vertex
    set_vertex_metadata, vertex_metadata, has_vertex_metadata
    delete_vertex_metadata

Edges and adjacency:

    add_edge_by_ids, add_edge_by_names
    has_edge_by_ids, has_edge_by_names
    delete_edge_by_ids, delete_edge_by_names
    add_edge, has_edge, delete_edge
    edges, edges_between, out_edges, in_edges
    out_neighbors, in_neighbors, out_degree, in_degree, edge_count

Every public edge result is an array reference ``[FROM_ID, TO_ID, LABEL]``.
The label is ``undef``, the empty string, or the registered string value.
``out_neighbors`` and ``in_neighbors`` contain one entry per edge, so a pair
of vertices joined by two differently labelled edges appears twice.

Labels:

    declare_label, declare_labels, labels, label_count

``declare_labels`` validates the complete list before changing the graph.  It
is therefore atomic when one of its arguments is invalid or would exceed the
compact label limit.

Algorithms:

    has_path, path, shortest_path
    descendants, strict_descendants, ancestors, strict_ancestors
    dfs_preorder, dfs_postorder, dfs_reverse_postorder
    topological_sort, is_acyclic
    source_vertices, sink_vertices, loop_vertices
    weakly_connected_components, strongly_connected_components
    strongly_connected_components_tarjan, strongly_connected_components_pearce
    strongly_connected_components_tarjan_zwick
    cyclic_strongly_connected_components
    cycle, shortest_cycle
    is_tree, is_arborescence, arborescence_root
    delete_paths, reachability_roots
    induced_subgraph, condensation

Folds:

    fold_vertices

Graphviz output:

    $g->to_dot(%options)
    $g->write_dot($path, %options)

The DOT writer is implemented by XGraph::IO::DOT and has no Graphviz
runtime dependency.  It emits a directed graph with stable v<ID> node
identifiers, names as vertex labels, and defined edge labels as DOT
label attributes.  undef labels are omitted and empty-string labels are
emitted explicitly.  show_ids => 1 appends numeric IDs to vertex labels.

BENCHMARKING
------------

The repository includes bench/graph-vs-xgraph.pl for direct comparisons with
the pure-Perl Graph distribution in the sibling ../Graph checkout.  Build
XGraph first, then run:

    perl bench/graph-vs-xgraph.pl

The benchmark uses the same integer vertices and deterministic DAG edge list
for both implementations.  It reports construction, edge lookup, outgoing
neighbors, reachability, edge enumeration, and topological-sort timings.
It also compares XGraph's Kosaraju, Tarjan, Pearce, and Tarjan--Zwick SCC
implementations against Graph's strongly connected-component implementation.
Graph's SCC cache is cleared before each timed call so repeated calls measure
the algorithm rather than a cached result.
The defaults are deliberately modest.  Set XGRAPH_BENCH_VERTICES,
XGRAPH_BENCH_EDGES, XGRAPH_BENCH_QUERIES, and XGRAPH_BENCH_ITERATIONS to
scale a run.  The reported ratio is Graph time divided by XGraph time.  This
is a timing comparison, not a memory benchmark, and cached algorithms should
be treated separately from uncached operations.

Benchmark snapshot (2026-09-21)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This snapshot was run at commit 5e30e51 on an 11th Gen Intel Core i7-1185G7
with Perl 5.36.3, the default compact XGraph build, and Graph 0.9735.  The
workload contained 2,000 integer vertices, 10,000 deterministic DAG edges,
1,000 queries, and five timed iterations.  The values below are total
wall-clock seconds across those iterations.  The final column is Graph time
divided by XGraph time.

    operation             XGraph       Graph  Graph/XGraph
    build                 0.011547    0.269821       23.37x
    has_edge              0.001043    0.011131       10.67x
    out_neighbors         0.001254    0.030077       23.98x
    all_successors        0.018452    2.623152      142.16x
    edges                 0.008157    0.010696        1.31x
    topological_sort      0.000696    1.037837     1491.27x
    scc_kosaraju          0.001127    2.322959     2061.18x
    scc_tarjan            0.001084    2.403516     2217.08x
    scc_pearce            0.001032    2.392471     2318.57x
    scc_tarjan_zwick      0.001494    2.445339     1636.59x

On this workload Pearce's implementation was fastest among the four XGraph
SCC implementations.  Tarjan--Zwick is retained as an explicit comparator;
this initial port does not beat Pearce on this particular graph shape.

These numbers are a baseline, not a hardware-independent guarantee.  They
measure topology operations with integer vertices and no edge payloads.  They
do not measure memory use, dense or hub-heavy graphs, wide mode, or threaded
execution.

For an optional comparison with SciPy's compiled sparse-graph implementation,
install SciPy in a Python environment and run:

    python bench/scipy-scc.py

The script uses the same SCC workload and reports SciPy's
``connected_components(..., connection="strong")`` timing separately from the
Perl benchmark.

For reference, SciPy 1.18.1 measured approximately 0.000520 seconds across
five iterations on the same host.  Relative to that timing, the XGraph SCC
rows above were approximately 2.17x (Kosaraju), 2.08x (Tarjan), 1.98x
(Pearce), and 2.87x (Tarjan--Zwick).  These are cross-process measurements,
so they should be treated as an algorithm-only snapshot rather than as a
definitive end-to-end ranking: SciPy returns flat labels while these XGraph
methods return grouped Perl arrays.

For native counter-based profiling, ``bench/profile-scc.pl`` builds the same
deterministic SCC workload once and repeats only the selected XGraph SCC
method.  For example:

    XGRAPH_PROFILE_ITERATIONS=1000 \
      perf stat -e cycles,instructions,cache-misses,branches,branch-misses \
      env PERL5LIB=blib/lib:blib/arch perl bench/profile-scc.pl

Set ``XGRAPH_PROFILE_METHOD`` to profile another SCC implementation.  When
comparing with SciPy, limit its optional numerical-library worker pools (for
example with ``OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1``) so that thread-pool startup does not
obscure the graph algorithm itself.  A profile on the reference workload
showed that removing Tarjan--Zwick's redundant member sort reduced its native
instruction count by roughly one quarter and its cycle count by roughly one
fifth over 200 calls; exact timings vary with system load.

End-to-end SCC comparison
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The algorithm-only comparison above does not have the same output contract as
XGraph.  For a fairer comparison, run both of these drivers:

    XGRAPH_BENCH_ITERATIONS=500 \
      PERL5LIB=blib/lib:blib/arch perl bench/e2e-scc-xgraph.pl

    OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 MKL_NUM_THREADS=1 \
      XGRAPH_BENCH_ITERATIONS=500 python bench/e2e-scc-scipy.py

Each iteration constructs a fresh graph, includes CSR construction on the
SciPy side, runs SCC once, converts SciPy's flat labels into sorted grouped
component lists, and releases the result.  The common edge list is prepared
before timing so Perl and Python are not being compared on their input-data
generators.

A three-run median on the reference host (2,000 vertices, 12,000 SCC edges,
500 fresh graphs per run) was approximately:

    implementation                 ms per construct/SCC/group
    XGraph Kosaraju                         4.01 ms
    XGraph Tarjan                           4.09 ms
    XGraph Pearce                           3.92 ms
    XGraph Tarjan--Zwick                    3.59 ms
    SciPy CSR + SCC + grouping              2.02 ms

Thus SciPy was approximately 1.8x faster than the fastest XGraph variant in
this end-to-end test, rather than the roughly 3x gap seen when comparing
prebuilt-graph SCC calls with
different result shapes.  The XGraph algorithm ordering is not stable enough
under ordinary system load to call these close timings a definitive ranking.
``fold_vertices`` invokes a callback with an ``XGraph::Vertex`` view, the
current accumulator, and an optional explicit context value.  The view
retains its graph and exposes the vertex ID, name, metadata, and adjacency
queries.

Algorithm results contain vertex IDs.  Collection results are array
references.  Paths are ordered from source to destination.  Cycles are closed
paths, so a self-loop is ``[$id, $id]``.  ``topological_sort`` returns
``undef`` for a cyclic graph.  See the module documentation for the exact
edge cases and ordering guarantees.

LAZY VERTEX ENUMERATION
-----------------------

The normal API is eager.  It returns complete array references and does not
use context to switch between scalar and lazy behavior.  For vertex
enumeration, ``vertex_iterator`` returns a Perl callable which asks an XS
cursor for the next ID:

    my $next = $g->vertex_iterator;
    while (defined(my $id = $next->())) {
        say $id;
    }

The callable returns ``undef`` after exhaustion.  A structural graph mutation
invalidates an active cursor and the next call throws.  The cursor retains a
reference to its graph, so the graph remains alive while the callable exists.
The cursor is an implementation detail of XGraph.  Planned generator
integration and frozen-store ithread sharing are not implemented yet.

ERRORS
------

Failures currently use ``croak`` with an ``XPERL.GRAPH.*`` error code.  The
important codes include:

    INVALID_ARGUMENT             malformed call or container
    INVALID_VERTEX_ID            malformed numeric ID
    UNKNOWN_VERTEX               well-formed but absent vertex
    DUPLICATE_VERTEX_NAME        name collision
    INVALID_LABEL                reference or invalid label input
    UNKNOWN_LABEL                undeclared label in strict mode
    LABEL_LIMIT                  compact label limit exceeded
    VERTEX_ID_EXHAUSTED          compact vertex-ID limit exceeded
    EDGE_EXISTS                  duplicate edge tuple
    CYCLE_REJECTED               acyclic graph would become cyclic
    FROZEN                       mutation attempted after freeze
    ITERATOR_INVALIDATED         cursor observed structural mutation

Predicate and deletion methods return false for ordinary absence where the
method documents that behavior.  Methods which require a live vertex throw
for an unknown vertex.  A malformed ID is never treated as ordinary absence.

THREADS AND PERL CORE INTEGRATION
---------------------------------

The graph class and cursor are non-cloneable with ``CLONE_SKIP``.  Frozen
native stores are not shared between ithreads.  XGraph uses an ordinary Perl
closure around an XS cursor.  It does not require or provide a new Perl-core
generator or iterator C API.

DOCUMENTATION MAP
-----------------

The installed module POD is the user-facing API reference:

    perldoc XGraph

The design and implementation record is in ``planning/``:

    planning/xgraph.md
        Normative data model, API contract, representation, ownership,
        algorithms, error behavior, and deferred work.
    planning/xgraph-implementation-packets.md
        Bounded implementation and validation packets.
    planning/xperl-graph-module-recommendations.md
        Rationale and comparison with the OTP graph model.
    planning/xgraph-discussion-summary.md
        Short summary of the design discussion.

The planning documents are excluded from release archives.  They remain in
the repository because they explain choices which are not visible in the
public API.

